INSERT INTO `content_pages` (`id`, `slug`, `title`, `content_markdown`, `show_in_nav`, `nav_label`, `nav_order`, `updated_at`, `visible_roles`) VALUES (1,'skills','Caving Skill Levels','NUCC uses the following skill levels for vertical caving progression. Each level has prerequisites and requires a sign-off from a qualified member.\r\n\r\n![NUCC Skills Flowchart 2026](/static/pages/skills-flowchart-2026.png)\r\n\r\n## Caving Skill Levels\r\n\r\n### Beginner Vertical Caver\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** None\r\n- **Signed off by:** Vertical Caver\r\n\r\n- Can safely prusik and abseil.\r\n- Can give a bottom/fireman\'s belay.\r\n- Shows clear vertical communication.\r\n- Can explain vertical safety basics (2 points of safety or a tested descender).\r\n- Understands fall factors and weighting devices to prevent slack.\r\n- Can identify, inspect and put on a basic club SRT kit.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate Vertical Caver\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Beginner Vertical Caver\r\n- **Signed off by:** Vertical Cave Leader\r\n\r\n- Demonstrates changeovers and rebelays and explains when/why they\'re used.\r\n- Keeps points of safety unsupervised.\r\n- Prusiks and abseils without supervision or belay.\r\n- Explains fall factors and slack prevention in caving.\r\n- Inspects and puts on their own SRT kit unsupervised.\r\n\r\n### Vertical Caver\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Intermediate Vertical Caver\r\n- **Signed off by:** Vertical Cave Leader\r\n\r\n- Demonstrates deviations/redirects and knot crossings.\r\n- Can teach basic caving skills (prusiking, abseiling, fireman\'s belay).\r\n- Basic rope problem-solving unsupervised.\r\n- Can inspect, disassemble and assemble their own SRT kit.\r\n- Can inspect club kits on others.\r\n\r\n### Knotcraft 1\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** None\r\n- **Signed off by:** Beginner Rigger\r\n\r\n- Ties, identifies and assesses Knotcraft 1 knots.\r\n- Explains their uses and limits.\r\n- Explains midline and endline knot principles.\r\n- Basic rope inspection for rub-points, wear and distorted core.\r\n\r\n**Knots:**\r\n- Figure eight on the bight\r\n- Figure nine on the bight\r\n- Overhand on the bight (tape)\r\n- Alpine butterfly\r\n- Tape/water knot (inline and on the bight)\r\n- Double fisherman\'s\r\n\r\n### Beginner Rigger\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Vertical Caver, Knotcraft 1\r\n- **Signed off by:** Vertical Cave Leader\r\n\r\n- Rigs a simple vertical cave on bolts or high-quality naturals.\r\n- Rigs simple rebelays.\r\n- Joins ropes on rope.\r\n- Explains backups, shockloading, carabiner types and loading, and rope-wear mitigation.\r\n- Rigs and isolates a ladder and belay.\r\n- Explains how to inspect bolts and anchors.\r\n\r\n### Horizontal Cave Leader\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Knotcraft 1, Vertical Caver\r\n- **Signed off by:** 2 Horizontal Trip Leaders & ratified by the Committee\r\n\r\nAssessed on leadership, behaviour, trip management, logistics, navigation, teaching and people skills (see the trip leader form).\r\n\r\n**Technical:**\r\n- Rig a ladder with a belay and isolate the pulley system.\r\n- Operate a belay on a ladder with a belay device.\r\n- Explain and rig handlines.\r\n- Select handline anchors.\r\n- Identify when a downclimb/handline would exceed \"horizontal\".\r\n- Gear planning.\r\n- First aid beneficial.\r\n- Summarise ASF MICC principles.\r\n\r\n### Knotcraft 2\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Beginner Rigger\r\n- **Signed off by:** Vertical Cave Leader\r\n\r\n- Ties, identifies and assesses Knotcraft 2 knots.\r\n- Explains their uses and limits.\r\n\r\n**Knots:**\r\n- Figure eight rethreaded\r\n- Munter hitch\r\n- European death knot\r\n- Barrel knot\r\n\r\n**Optional:**\r\n- Fusion knot\r\n- Clove hitch\r\n- Bowline on the bight\r\n- Tensionless hitch\r\n- Prusik knot\r\n- Stone knot\r\n\r\n### Rigger\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Knotcraft 2\r\n- **Signed off by:** Vertical Cave Leader\r\n\r\n- Rigs complex vertical caves (multiple rebelays off naturals, redirects).\r\n- Rigs approach lines and understands edge protection.\r\n- Explains rope wear and management.\r\n- Assesses and equalises anchors.\r\n- Gear planning and use of rigging notes/cave maps.\r\n- Understands ropework styles.\r\n- Selects rope thickness, length and gear.\r\n- Describes carabiner styles (HMS, oval).\r\n\r\n### Party Rescue\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Knotcraft 2, Beginner Rigger\r\n- **Signed off by:** Vertical Cave Leader\r\n\r\n*Description work in progress.*\r\n\r\n- Forces, mechanical advantage and efficiency.\r\n- Executing standard intra-party rescue techniques (pickoff, counterbalance haul, 3:1 z-haul, improvised belay).\r\n- Lateral thinking and improvisation.\r\n- Familiarity with HMPE (dyneema), nylon and polyester rope and tape.\r\n\r\n### Vertical Cave Leader\r\n\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Party Rescue, Rigger, Horizontal Cave Leader\r\n- **Signed off by:** 2 Vertical Cave Leaders & ratified by the Committee\r\n\r\n*Description work in progress.*\r\n\r\n- Summarise ASF MICC principles and know where to find more.\r\n\r\n## Guidelines for Further Skill Development\r\n\r\nAdditional areas of skill development recognised by NUCC. Descriptions are a work in progress.\r\n\r\n### Surveyor\r\n\r\n- **Signed off by:** Surveyor\r\n\r\nNo description given on the source page.\r\n\r\n### Bolter\r\n\r\n- **Signed off by:** Bolter\r\n\r\n- Requires Rigger.\r\n- Describes anchor placement theory, bolting hardware and techniques, ethics and forces.\r\n- Practical knowledge of at least two of:\r\n  - Glue-ins\r\n  - Expansion bolts\r\n  - Concrete screws\r\n  - Removables (e.g. Coeur Pulse)\r\n- Metal grades (316 SS, 304 SS, zinc plated).\r\n\r\n### Cave Rescue\r\n\r\n- Group rescue ability:\r\n  - Stretcher rescues\r\n  - Complex hauls\r\n  - Guided ascents/descents\r\n  - 9:1 hauls\r\n  - Team communication\r\n- Advanced forces and gear rating (WLL, MBS) understanding.\r\n- First aid and bolting experience highly regarded.\r\n- External organisations:\r\n  - NSW CRS\r\n  - CRV\r\n\r\n### Karst Conservation\r\n\r\n*Description work in progress.*\r\n\r\n### Cave Exploration\r\n\r\n*Description work in progress.*\r\n\r\n### Cave Science\r\n\r\n*Description work in progress.*\r\n\r\n*See also: the [NUCC Skills Flowchart 2026 PDF](https://nucc.caves.org.au/NUCC_Skills_Flowchart_2026.pdf) and the [SRT Details](/pages/srt-details) page.*',1,'Skills',0,'2026-11-11 17:50:21',NULL),
(2,'srt-details','SRT Details','\n**Warning:** SRT is potentially lethal. No one in NUCC is formally qualified to teach it, and you should not try to learn it from the internet alone. This page is not a full ropework guide.\n\nFor a proper grounding, see *Vertical* by Al Warild, and *Alpine Caving Techniques* by Marbach and Tourte.\n\nCanberra-area caves have moderate vertical work -- the largest single Australian pitch is Bungonia, at about 60m. Ladders usually stop and SRT starts around 9m (30ft).\n\n## Topics\n\n- [SRT Kit](/pages/srt-kit)\n- [Abseiling](/pages/srt-abseil)\n- [Prusiking](/pages/srt-prusik)\n- [Belaying](/pages/srt-belay)\n- [Changeovers](/pages/srt-changeover)\n- [Rebelays](/pages/srt-rebelay)\n- [Redirects](/pages/srt-redirect)\n- [Rigging knots](/pages/srt-knot)\n- [Knot crossing](/pages/srt-knotcross)\n- [Basic rigging](/pages/srt-basicrig)\n- [Rescue techniques](/pages/srt-rescue)\n- [Final comments](/pages/srt-conclusion)\n',1,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:55',NULL),
(3,'srt-kit','SRT Kit','\nNUCC\'s roughly dozen standardised club SRT sets use the following gear, and why.\n\n## Descenders\n- **SRTE aluminium 5-bar U-frame rack** — the club\'s main descender.\n- **Aspiring stainless 5-bar U-frame rack** — gradually replacing the aluminium racks.\n- **Petzl Simple** and **Petzl Stop** — members may use these, but the club prefers U-frame racks because they lock off easily and suit beginners.\n\n## Ascenders\nPetzl **Ascension** and Petzl **Croll** only — the older, larger Croll model, since the small model has design flaws. No foot ascenders (Petzl Pantin) are used, since they wear out the chest ascender faster.\n\n## Sit harness\nMostly Petzl **Fractio**, plus one MTDE Amazonia I and one Petzl Superavanti.\n\n## Cowstails\nStandard double cowstails with locking carabiners, in 9 to 10.5mm dynamic rope. Current ones are about 10mm; newer ones 9.8mm.\n\n## Connectors, Chest Harness, Foot Loop\n*(No additional notes on these yet.)*\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(4,'srt-abseil','Abseiling','\nAbseiling is descending a rope with a friction descender. NUCC prefers the **Rack**, and also uses Bobbins and Stops. Figure-eight descenders and ATCs are not used, since they must be removed from the harness to get on rope.\n\n## Standard drill\n1. Clip the long cowstail to the rigging krab or safety point.\n2. Approach the pitch head and call **\"ON ROPE\"**, waiting for **\"ON BELAY\"**.\n3. Thread the descender as high as possible.\n4. Go to half-lock, then full lock.\n5. Climb out and settle.\n6. Test by easing 3 to 5cm of rope.\n7. Return to full lock.\n8. Remove the long cowstail.\n9. Call **\"ABSEILING\"**, waiting for **\"ABSEIL WHEN READY\"**.\n10. Move from half-lock into the abseil position.\n11. Descend.\n12. At the bottom, remove the descender and call **\"OFF ROPE\"**.\n\n**Warning:** with Racks, do not let go of the rope, or you can go into free-fall.\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(5,'srt-prusik','Prusiking','\nPrusiking is climbing back up a rope, using mechanical ascenders rather than prusik loops. NUCC runs weekly SRT sessions to practise it.\n\n**Warning:** always keep two ascenders on the rope. Never prusik with one.\n\nThe standard **frog system** uses chest and hand ascenders. Europeans add a foot ascender; Americans use ropewalking.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Start as high as is safe.\n2. Clip the chest ascender and tension it.\n3. Clip the hand ascender at about head height and unclip the footloop.\n4. Sit with both feet in the footloop.\n5. Stand to raise the chest ascender, then sit and slide the hand ascender up.\n6. Repeat.\n7. Before climbing past the top, clip a long cowstail to a safety point above.\n8. Remove the chest ascender first, then the hand ascender, and call **\"OFF ROPE\"**.\n\n## Notes\n- A weighted bag, or someone pulling the rope below, makes climbing easier.\n- The hand ascender is tethered to the harness with a dynamic lanyard and maillon; the footloop has its own maillon and clip.\n- Adjust the footloop so the top of the hand ascender sits at about belly-button height when standing in it.\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(6,'srt-belay','Belaying','\nBelays are the exception for club SRT. Top-rope belays are almost always unsuitable: loose debris, friction in good descenders, poor sightlines, rope stretch on long pitches, and rebelays that prevent belaying below them.\n\nThe first person down usually abseils without a belay. If one is needed, they may use an autolocking descender such as a Stop, which is not a beginner\'s tool.\n\nBeginners get a bottom (**\"fireman\'s\"**) belay. The belayer holds the rope with both hands and watches the abseiler. When help is needed, they grip the rope firmly and move away from the pitch to tighten it. If the abseiler is unconscious, lower them carefully.\n\n## Caveats\n- Stand close to but not under the pitch.\n- Practise first in a controlled setting.\n- Do not anchor the rope to the belayer.\n- With heavy rope stretch, use a more elaborate belay.\n\n**Note:** a top belay may be justified in rare cases, such as foul air needing rapid extraction, though better options usually exist.\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(7,'srt-changeover','Changeovers','\nA changeover moves between abseiling and prusiking while staying on rope. It is the first self-rescue technique, for problems such as hair or clothing caught in the descender, foul air, or a waterfall.\n\n**Stay calm.**\n\n## Down-to-up (more common)\n1. Lock the descender fully.\n2. Clip the hand ascender and footloop at about head height.\n3. Stand and clip the chest ascender high.\n4. Prusik up three times on each ascender to check.\n5. Release the descender from lock and remove it.\n6. Continue up.\n\n## Up-to-down\n1. Move the chest ascender close to the hand ascender.\n2. Kink the descender so it sits close to where the rope exits the chest ascender.\n3. Lock it fully.\n4. Stand to unweight and remove the chest ascender.\n5. Sit back and check you can reach the hand ascender.\n6. Test the descender with minimal rope run; re-lock.\n7. Remove the hand ascender and continue down.\n\n**Warning:** do not remove the hand ascender until it has been tested with three small prusiks (see the Swiss Cheese Model).\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(8,'srt-rebelay','Rebelays','\nRebelays are used in alpine-style rigging to protect thin rope and formations and let several people descend together. Each one is a new pitch head, with extra anchors if needed.\n\n## Going down\n1. Descend to the rebelay anchor karabiner.\n2. Lock off and clip the short cowstail to it.\n3. Clip the long cowstail above the descender and continue into the rebelay loop until the short cowstail takes the weight.\n4. Remove the descender, rethread it below the rebelay on the anchor side, and lock it.\n5. Step into the loop (or use the hand ascender and footloop if the loop is too small) and unclip the short cowstail.\n6. Test the rack; return to full lock.\n7. Remove the long cowstail and continue.\n\n## Going up\n1. Prusik to just below the rebelay knot without jamming the ascender on it.\n2. Clip the long cowstail to the anchor karabiner (a different cowstail from the descent).\n3. Stand in the footloop, take the up rope with the left hand, and open and clip the chest ascender with the right.\n4. Sit back and shift weight to the up rope, keeping the chest ascender out of the bottom of the loop.\n5. Move the hand ascender across and prusik both ascenders at least three times.\n6. Unclip the long cowstail and continue.\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(9,'srt-redirect','Redirects','\nAlso called **deviations** or **redirections**. A redirect is a simplified rebelay: faster to cross, but it does not create a new pitch, so it cannot be used as an anchor or fully weighted.\n\n## Going down\n1. Descend to level with or just above the redirect anchor and lock off. Optionally clip the short cowstail.\n2. Hold the loop with both hands, one at the karabiner base and the other near the anchor.\n3. Open the gate with one hand and do not let go.\n4. Pull toward the anchor, then clip the karabiner back around the rope above the descender.\n5. Remove the full lock and continue.\n\n## Going up\n1. Prusik to just below the karabiner and keep pushing it up.\n2. When the attachment is nearly fully tensioned, take the loop in both hands and remove the karabiner from the rope without letting go.\n3. Clip the redirect around the rope below the lowest ascender and keep climbing.\n\n**Warning:** never let go of the redirect, since it may be protecting you from a serious hazard.\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(10,'srt-knot','Rigging knots','\nThis page does not teach knot tying — see online guides for that. Have an experienced caver check your knots before trusting them.\n\n**Suggested minimal set:** Figure Eight, Tape, Double Fisherman\'s, Munter Hitch, and Alpine Butterfly.\n\n## Figure Nine\nThe most important rigging knot, and NUCC\'s primary knot for attaching rope to anchors. The Figure Eight is \"good enough\" for most cases.\n\n## Rethreaded Figure Eight\nA quick way to attach a rope to a back-up anchor, but it uses more rope and is harder on the anchor and rope. Tape is preferred.\n\n## Alpine Butterfly\nThe mid-rope knot, used for pull-through anchors, Y-hangs, isolating damaged rope, and bolt traverses. Suggested for anchoring all rebelays. Several look-alike variants exist.\n\n## Tape (Water) Knot\nJoins tape into a sling. Each tail should be at least 10cm, tails exit on opposite sides, and the tape should lie flat and not twist.\n\n## European Death Knot\nJoins ropes, but is weaker than the Double Fisherman\'s. Recommended only when the rope will be pulled down and may jam.\n\n## Also used (no detailed notes yet)\nDouble Fisherman\'s Knot, Fusion Knot, Bowline on a Bight, Stone Knot, Munter Hitch, and Prusik Knot.\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(11,'srt-knotcross','Knot crossing','\nFirst ask whether a crossing is really needed: could another rope avoid it, or a rebelay be placed to avoid it? If not, use the procedure below (adapted from *Vertical*).\n\n## Going down (an extended changeover)\n1. Descend close to the knot; clip the long cowstail to the safety knot.\n2. Attach the hand ascender.\n3. Stand and attach the chest ascender.\n4. Move the descender to just below the joining knot and lock it, making sure you are on the correct rope.\n5. Downprusik both ascenders close to the knot.\n6. Remove the chest ascender; weight the descender; remove the hand ascender.\n7. Test the descender; remove the cowstail and continue.\n\nAnyone attempting this should already be competent at changeovers.\n\n## Going up\n1. Prusik to just below the knot.\n2. Clip the long cowstail into the passing loop (the short cowstail is an alternative).\n3. Stand, move the chest ascender above the joining knot.\n4. Move the hand ascender past the knot the same way.\n5. Prusik up three times; remove the cowstail and continue.\n\n## Joining ropes at a rebelay\n1. Rig the rebelay on the upper rope with a Figure Nine.\n2. Tie a stopper knot in the lower end of the upper rope.\n3. Tie a rethreaded Figure Eight from the lower rope through the upper rope\'s loop at the rebelay krab.\n4. Rethread the krab through both loops.\n5. Lower the lower rope with a stopper knot; continue.\n\nIf the rebelay fails, establish a new anchor rather than relying on the knots.\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(12,'srt-basicrig','Basic rigging','\nRigging must be learned from experienced people and checked by a second person. It is impossible to cover every situation, and there is **no shame** in declining to rig something.\n\n## Principles, in order of importance\nSafe, redundant, equalised, efficient, effective, retrievable.\n\n## Steps\n1. Find a live, solidly rooted tree or bedrock feature over 30cm in diameter; wrap tape around it three times and join with a tape knot.\n2. Equalise the two tape loops, clip a karabiner across them, put a Figure Nine in one end of the rope and clip it in, and put a Figure Eight stopper in the other end.\n3. Take the rope to the pitch head and attach it to the two main anchors with Figure Nines.\n4. Throw, lower, or feed the rope down.\n5. Descend.\n6. Enjoy the cave.\n7. Afterwards, ask others for feedback.\n\n## Tips\n- How you dress the knot matters.\n- Keep angles between knots under 90 degrees where possible and never beyond 125.\n- A rope rated to 20kN does not make a 1kN load safe.\n- Three poor anchors do not equal one good one.\n- Watch for rub points.\n- Avoid bowlines.\n- Garden the pitch head before rigging.\n- Rebelays make joining ropes and crossing knots easier.\n- **Don\'t forget the stopper knot!**\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(13,'srt-rescue','Rescue techniques','\n*(This section is a placeholder on the source page — no detailed content yet.)*\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL),
(14,'srt-conclusion','Final comments','\nThis guide omits much of SRT — keep learning. Other caving skills worth learning include horizontal caving, surveying, geomorphology, history, bolting, expedition and exploration caving, navigation, conservation, and digging.\n\n*Go and have a great time on rope and underground!*\n\n\n[← back to SRT Details](/pages/srt-details)\n',0,NULL,0,'2026-11-11 17:53:32',NULL);
